---
import Answer from "../../components/challenge/Answer.astro";
import ChallengeIntro from "../../components/challenge/ChallengeIntro.astro";
import Checklist from "../../components/challenge/Checklist.astro";
import Message from "../../components/challenge/Message.astro";
import Task from "../../components/challenge/Task.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Document a Vendor API - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="exploring-ai-capabilities" subChapterId="document-api">
		<ChallengeIntro skill="The agent can work out how a vendor API really behaves, by reading what docs exist and calling the API to check them, then write it up.">
			Claims want to order drone surveys from Skyreach Surveys. Before anyone builds against it, the team needs documentation it
			can trust. All you have is the vendor's welcome email.
		</ChallengeIntro>

		<Message kind="email" subject="Skyreach API access" from="Marco, Partner Success, Skyreach Surveys" to="Kaiju Mutual Claims" date="Tue, 29 Sep 2026 11:20">
			<p>Hi team,</p>
			<p>Great to have Kaiju Mutual on board! Your sandbox is ready.</p>
			<p>
				Base URL: https://skyreach-api.konrad.dev<br />
				API key: skr-live-91c04b77 (send it in the X-Api-Key header)
			</p>
			<p>
				Everything you need is on our developer site at https://skyreach.konrad.dev. In short, you order a survey for a property
				and we fly it and send you the damage report. Most clients give us a callback URL so they don't have to keep checking.
			</p>
			<p>Shout if you get stuck!</p>
			<p>Marco<br />Partner Success, Skyreach Surveys</p>
		</Message>

		<Task>Write a prompt to have your agent write documentation for the Skyreach API that you can build against, ideally an OpenAPI (Swagger) file.</Task>

		<Checklist
			items={[
				"Read Skyreach's developer site.",
				"Call the API to check what the site says, and list every place they disagree.",
				"Create a survey and follow it until it finishes.",
				"Produce an OpenAPI file covering every endpoint, with real request and response examples.",
				"Write prose for what OpenAPI can't describe well, such as timing and callbacks.",
				"Document the error format.",
			]}
		/>

		<Answer
			prompt={`Skyreach sent us this email: <email>

Write documentation for their API that we can build against: an OpenAPI file, plus a markdown page for anything OpenAPI can't describe well. Put both in docs/vendors/skyreach/.

Their developer site may be out of date, so don't trust it on its own. Check every claim it makes against the real API: call each endpoint, try the fields and values the site lists, create a survey and poll it until it finishes, and record what actually happens. List every place the site and the API disagree.`}
		>
			<p>
				An agent that only turns the docs site into Swagger fails this one. The site documents v1, and the API is v2. Check the
				result for the v2 paths, the real field names and priority values, all six statuses including <code>aborted</code> and{" "}
				<code>cancelled</code>, the timing (about 90 seconds, 45 when urgent), the 0 to 100 damage score, the error shape,
				cancelling with <code>DELETE</code>, and a callback that is sent once and never retried.
			</p>
			<p>
				The questions to ask the agent: did it create a survey and poll it to the end, and did it try what the site says against
				the API? The site's changelog, its FAQ and an HTML comment in the page source all give parts of the truth away.
			</p>
			<p>
				Everyone shares one sandbox key, so the list endpoint shows other trainees' surveys too. A callback can't be tested from a
				laptop without a public request bin. The FAQ is enough to document it.
			</p>
		</Answer>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
